#
# THE PREDICTION FOR runtime-2's RE-MEASURE, stated BEFORE the run so it can falsify rather than confirm.
# In the csr_warl block, after this landing:
#   cr_mml_exec_suppress's four bins (rlb0_c1001/c1010/c1011/c1101) CANNOT move. They need encodings this
#     landing does not touch. If any of them moves, this fix reached something it should not have.
#   cp_outcome.ignored_mml_exec (13 of 39 before) may LOSE only seeds whose ONLY such sample was a c1111.
#   cr_lock_outcome.locked_rlb0_ignored (23 of 39 before) may GAIN those and cannot lose any.
#   cr_mml_nonexec_accept.c1111_written (3 of 39 before) must hold or rise: it is fed by writes that LAND,
#     which the guard never touched.
#   cr_self_lock.locked_rlb1_written and cr_tor_lock.nl_tor_rlb1_written (0 of 39 and 0 of 40 before) become
#     REACHABLE for the first time; whether they are HIT depends on the stimulus reaching that combination.
#   cr_top_lock.top_unlocked_written (40 of 40 in the lock entry) must HOLD. gen_pmp_lock_prog.py's
#     p6_top_entry asserts the top entry is unlocked, writes pmpaddr15 one to three times with must_change
#     set, and only then locks it, so its samples are RAW-unlocked and never depended on RLB.
#
# AND THE PREDICTION'S LIMIT, said before the run rather than after. cross_auto_bin_max is 0, so an unnamed
# {mml1, rlb0, c1111, ignored_mml_exec} tuple is counted NOWHERE and the only visible trace of the Minor-1
# misroute is a seed's membership in cp_outcome.ignored_mml_exec. A seed that also had a legitimate
# exec-encoding sample shows nothing. So "no number moved" is a POSSIBLE AND CORRECT outcome, not a failed
# fix. The proof of these changes is the classifier reading what the RTL reads; the numbers can only falsify.
